We think that VR as something that's
going to enable a new kind of connection.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.100
So much so that we have a thesis that if
VR is going to be successful commercially,

00:14:15.100 --> 00:14:17.650
it will be because people are connecting.

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:20.770
If it's going to be something
that's well beyond Gamers and

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:24.020
some entertainment,
it's because people are feeling

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.710
an emotional connection that
wasn't available for them before.

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:31.770
So the question that we ask
ourselves is what do we get from VR?

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:35.305
What are the things about VR
that makes it a new medium.

00:14:35.305 --> 00:14:38.493
[NOISE] And
when I started researching this space.

00:14:38.493 --> 00:14:42.285
It started with the neuroscience
of connection, and

00:14:42.285 --> 00:14:48.430
how our brains would use VR and reading
things like professor Valenson works on.

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:51.220
And VR if nothing else,
is really a tool for

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.460
fooling your brain into
thinking you're someplace else.

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.940
And so if we can fool you to
think your someplace else,

00:14:56.940 --> 00:14:58.820
we can fool you to think you're
there with other people.

00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:03.980
And so then the question is, what is it
that we get from being actually physically

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:08.420
present with other people that we don't
get in electronic communications media?

00:15:08.420 --> 00:15:12.570
What is it that's different about this
experience of sitting in a room together

00:15:12.570 --> 00:15:15.360
then watching this on YouTube watchroom?

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:18.350
How different is the experience that
your having then that they're having?

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:24.690
And you could argue that well they
see the same guy in the blue sweater

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.310
saying the same words at the same time.

00:15:26.310 --> 00:15:29.990
Actually 30 or
45 seconds later probably, right?

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.730
So you could argue that the information
conveyed is largely similar.

00:15:33.730 --> 00:15:36.920
But I think you would never make
the argument that sitting in the audience

00:15:36.920 --> 00:15:40.850
at a Ted Talk is the same as
watching a TED Talk on YouTube.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:44.410
That sitting in the audience
at a comedy show is the same

00:15:44.410 --> 00:15:46.900
as watching a comedy special on Netflix.

00:15:46.900 --> 00:15:51.135
That being at a concert is the same
as listening to a CD of a concert.

00:15:51.135 --> 00:15:54.115
Are that, being in the same room
with your family is the same as

00:15:54.115 --> 00:15:56.775
being on video chat with your family,
I think you'd never make that.

00:15:56.775 --> 00:15:59.105
So when you think about that problem and
say, decompose.

00:15:59.105 --> 00:16:02.485
What is it that we get when we're
physically together that we don't

00:16:02.485 --> 00:16:03.915
have on these electronic media.

00:16:04.950 --> 00:16:05.800
Well, it's things like this.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:10.156
I can chose which one of you
I'm interacting with and

00:16:10.156 --> 00:16:11.448
what [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Sorry.

00:16:11.448 --> 00:16:12.603
&gt;&gt; That's okay.

00:16:12.603 --> 00:16:17.210
[LAUGH] How we feel when we interact,
when we make eye contact,

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:20.060
is dramatically different than
how we can feel on video chat.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.185
Our ability to change the distance of
how far we are from each other connotes

00:16:24.185 --> 00:16:25.177
something, hi.

00:16:25.177 --> 00:16:26.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right?

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:31.390
We feel that sense of physical space
that we don't have on video chat.

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:35.100
I have the ability to draw your
attention to something and

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.310
to know if you're looking at it.

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:40.277
So this is all what we talk about
as non-verbal communication.

00:16:40.277 --> 00:16:43.130
You're all intimately aware of the people
who are to your left and right,

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:44.520
even if don't interact with them.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:45.870
Even if you're not talking to them,

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.710
you're not looking at them,
you know there's somebody there.

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.950
And the feeling of being in this crowd,
that emotion that comes from that,

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:54.495
the connection that comes from
that is something that you don't

00:16:54.495 --> 00:16:58.520
have in electronic communications media,
but you do have in virtual reality.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:01.513
So the first thing that we really get
when we're together that we also get in

00:17:01.513 --> 00:17:03.557
virtual reality that we
don't have in video chat,

00:17:03.557 --> 00:17:06.240
that we don't have in phone calls
is non-verbal communication.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:10.750
This way to communicate we call more
naturally than you can communicate

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:12.035
otherwise online.

00:17:12.035 --> 00:17:15.593
Okay, the second big thing you get in
VR that you don't have in video chat or

00:17:15.593 --> 00:17:18.760
audio chat is what we call
the context of communication.

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.706
What can we do together?

00:17:20.706 --> 00:17:23.196
You think about video chat, you pretty
much just stare at each other and talk.

00:17:23.196 --> 00:17:24.670
Actually, you stare kind of at each other.

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:26.850
Mostly you look at your picture in
the bottom right hand corner, right?

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:29.836
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Through a phone call,

00:17:29.836 --> 00:17:31.590
all you can do is talk, okay?

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.490
You can maybe find a counter-example
in video games or something.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:35.980
You can talk while you play a video game.

00:17:35.980 --> 00:17:38.020
But most of the time,
when we get together socially,

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.840
we get together from a professional
setting, there's something we're doing

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:43.640
together that forms a centerpiece
of that communication.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.010
So you think about when you
get together with friends.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:48.923
You play a card game, you cook,
you throw a Frisbee, you go for a walk,

00:17:48.923 --> 00:17:49.976
you watch a movie.

00:17:49.976 --> 00:17:51.809
Think about when you get there,
in a business setting,

00:17:51.809 --> 00:17:53.876
you're going to have a white board,
you're going to have slides,

00:17:53.876 --> 00:17:54.820
some kind of presentation.

00:17:54.820 --> 00:17:56.570
You have documents you're reviewing.

00:17:56.570 --> 00:17:59.820
There's some media, there's something
that forms the centerpiece of most of our

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:01.500
communications experiences.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:05.360
And most of the time, electronically,
that's really terrible.

00:18:05.360 --> 00:18:08.696
You're in there trying to do
a whiteboarding session on Skype or

00:18:08.696 --> 00:18:09.820
on Google Hangouts.

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:11.270
It's a miserable experience.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:15.901
And this is why we fly, as I once did,
18 hours each way to Santiago, Chile, for

00:18:15.901 --> 00:18:17.396
a 90-minute meeting.

00:18:17.396 --> 00:18:19.775
It absurd, it's absurd.

00:18:19.775 --> 00:18:22.575
If you think about, really think about
why we travel all this distance,

00:18:22.575 --> 00:18:23.755
it's for simple things like that.

00:18:23.755 --> 00:18:27.076
For non-verbal communication and for the
ability to have context in communication.

00:18:27.076 --> 00:18:31.252
So in VR we see as this medium where
we start to get this stuff back.

00:18:31.252 --> 00:18:32.612
And that's really exciting.

00:18:32.612 --> 00:18:35.579
So we think about what this is
going to be, that's what we focus on.

00:18:35.579 --> 00:18:38.677
Now I gather you all visited
the Virtual Human Interaction Lab today,

00:18:38.677 --> 00:18:40.582
which is awesome, the VR Lab at Stanford.

00:18:40.582 --> 00:18:43.222
We only started calling it the VR Lab
a few years ago when VR got cool.

00:18:43.222 --> 00:18:44.412
It used to be the VHIL.

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:49.810
One of the things I'll
touch upon because it's so

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:53.730
core to this concept of connecting,
and I know you were just there,

00:18:53.730 --> 00:18:57.080
is how people think about
communicating in virtual spaces.

00:18:57.080 --> 00:18:59.114
because I put on this headset, right?

00:18:59.114 --> 00:19:01.600
And I look around this room,
and I see other people.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:05.610
But I don't see photorealistic
representations of people, I see avatars.

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:08.641
And that consciously, for
most people, when they hear that,

00:19:08.641 --> 00:19:12.643
when they see pictures of that, they might
see videos of that, they think, gosh,

00:19:12.643 --> 00:19:14.032
I'm not going to like that.

00:19:14.032 --> 00:19:16.535
I don't think I personally,
[SOUND] you can stand up on stage and

00:19:16.535 --> 00:19:18.719
tell me you're going to
feel emotionally connected.

00:19:18.719 --> 00:19:21.740
But I don't think I personally am going to
be emotionally connected by that.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:25.100
It's a really fascinating piece
of research that they've done.

00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:27.080
They've done tons of
studies on this stuff.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:29.210
And the way I would summarize it for
you, and

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:32.470
you can suspend your disbelief until
you have experiences around this.

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:36.620
Is that we consciously expect that we're
not going to enjoy these experiences and

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:40.460
not be connected with
these people as avatars.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:42.710
But subconsciously,
when you measure what happens,

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.365
when you actually get into the experience,
and you see people's responses.

00:19:45.365 --> 00:19:49.340
And you objectively measure it,
rather than subjectively measure it.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:52.490
They are connected, and in fact they can
be more connected than when I talk on

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.270
video chat and
I kind of can't make eye contact with you.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:58.000
And so even when those avatars
are tremendously abstract,

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:01.580
as you'll see spheres with two dots for
eyes, okay?

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:04.380
And if that sphere with two dots for
eyes gets really close to you,

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:05.660
you're going to feel like
there's a person there.

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:09.180
So since you were there,
I wanted to touch upon that, but-

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:10.260
&gt;&gt; And not just people.

00:20:10.260 --> 00:20:15.430
The reason I put this video up, this is me
playing in Chris Milk's latest creation,

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:18.260
Life With Us, at Sundance last week.

00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:22.430
And I'm playing with them, and Greenberg,
who started Gracenote, next to me.

00:20:22.430 --> 00:20:24.735
So we're both in this experience, and

00:20:24.735 --> 00:20:29.230
Chris takes you on a journey
from an egg to adulthood.

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:32.020
And takes you through a number
of different characters.

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:35.787
So instantly, he throws you
off a cliff and you're flying.

00:20:35.787 --> 00:20:39.690
And within a second,
I watched several people play it.

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:43.330
Within a second, everybody got,
wait a second, I have wings, I can fly?

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:44.534
I'm flying.

00:20:44.534 --> 00:20:47.704
And you have empathy for
that character, and

00:20:47.704 --> 00:20:50.990
you start behaving like that character.

00:20:50.990 --> 00:20:55.066
You start running when
you're a dinosaur or an ape.

00:20:55.066 --> 00:20:57.882
I think I'm an ape, right now,
with a monkey on my back.

00:20:57.882 --> 00:20:59.535
[LAUGH] Which is sort of funny.

00:20:59.535 --> 00:21:01.590
You can grab the monkey and
throw him off, right?

00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:05.030
Grab the monkey off your friend, who's
playing with you, and throw them off.

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:07.550
But you're quickly get into that.

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:11.790
And the voices he is pushing
back at you are your own voices.

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:16.730
But changed to the character that
you're seeing on the screen in VR.

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:17.990
So it's quite fascinating.

00:21:17.990 --> 00:21:23.560
And it show you the state of the art of
social behaviors in a VR setting, right?

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:24.251
&gt;&gt; This is one of this things.

00:21:24.251 --> 00:21:26.690
I've been doing this for four years now.

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:30.940
And I have this experience over and over
again where I can talk until I'm blue in

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:35.090
the face about how you're going to feel
in situations like this or in social VR.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:36.560
And people say, yeah, yeah, I get it.

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:39.810
And then 90 seconds into
actually having that experience,

00:21:39.810 --> 00:21:41.540
how they feel is dramatically transformed.

00:21:41.540 --> 00:21:45.008
So suspend your disbelief and
enjoy, thanks for having me.

00:21:45.008 --> 00:21:49.500
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah!

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:56.900
So Meron, God,
how do I explain how I met you?

00:21:56.900 --> 00:22:04.024
The first time that I met him, he had 30
nerds up in this mansion up in Woodside.

00:22:04.024 --> 00:22:08.771
With a tank collection in the backyard
which it was there from the guy who owned

00:22:08.771 --> 00:22:10.830
the mansion back then.

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:11.397
And-
&gt;&gt; Actually,

00:22:11.397 --> 00:22:14.860
the largest private collection
of military tanks in the world.

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:18.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, not any more because
the family sold them all off.

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:23.241
But his team was building
software to study gestures.

00:22:23.241 --> 00:22:27.308
And it took me back to my time at
Microsoft when I got a tour of

00:22:27.308 --> 00:22:29.370
Microsoft Research.

00:22:29.370 --> 00:22:30.560
I worked there for three years.

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.950
And Andy Wilson had a lab there and

00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:38.590
was showing me how his algorithms
would see your fingers closing.

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:43.380
And would give you a control surface
on those fingers, gestures, right?

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:47.650
And you could do things with your hands
to zoom in on maps and stuff like that.

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:51.572
And you guys took that to a whole
new level and impressed me a lot.

00:22:51.572 --> 00:22:55.517
And you're still on this mission
because your mission is to bring us

00:22:55.517 --> 00:22:58.360
these glasses that'll do wondrous things.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:00.309
So take it away, and I'll turn this off,

00:23:00.309 --> 00:23:02.551
otherwise it'll be very distractive for
you.

00:23:02.551 --> 00:23:06.431
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK] and thanks for
having me everyone.

00:23:06.431 --> 00:23:09.734
So [COUGH] I'm want to tell you
a little bit about my company.

00:23:09.734 --> 00:23:13.897
About why we're building
an operating system for

00:23:13.897 --> 00:23:16.877
the mind, for augmented reality.

00:23:16.877 --> 00:23:20.560
What that kind of an operating
system might look like.

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:24.990
And what's our focus,
as it compares to everyone else here.

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:28.800
You're hearing a lot about augmented and
virtual reality, I'm sure.

00:23:28.800 --> 00:23:31.670
So let's try and
make sense of all of this.

00:23:32.700 --> 00:23:36.960
So we build augmented reality
glasses that are very immersive.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:44.780
They allow you to see pretty much
photorealistic imagery in space.

00:23:44.780 --> 00:23:48.580
And you have this large
screen to be able to see it.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:50.909
But you also see the real
world in the background.

00:23:50.909 --> 00:23:53.770
And you can touch these
holograms with your very hands.

00:23:53.770 --> 00:23:56.680
You can interact collaboratively and
that kind of thing.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:23:59.780
So that's the basics of what Meta does,

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:02.050
very similar to
the Microsoft HoloLens in some ways.

00:24:03.900 --> 00:24:07.730
However, unlike other people out there,
we're really

00:24:07.730 --> 00:24:12.490
kind of obsessed with building
the most optimal operating system.

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:15.400
That really feels like
an extension of your imagination.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:17.810
Because the power in our minds,
of holograms,

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:20.150
is to be able to simplify computing.

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:23.680
In essence we believe that your Iron Man,

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:30.660
Tony Stark workspace can allow you to
create a much easier computing experience.

00:24:30.660 --> 00:24:35.764
If I'm surrounded by 3D models
that work the way my brain does.

00:24:35.764 --> 00:24:37.713
In 3D and I can collaborate with you and

00:24:37.713 --> 00:24:40.230
we can pass each other files back and
forth.

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:44.190
And I might have some supplementary
panel information that we can discuss.

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:49.160
And I might have a 3D Skype person
kind of holographically telepresence

00:24:49.160 --> 00:24:53.640
into the room and have a natural
discussion the way we are here.

00:24:53.640 --> 00:24:55.830
And computing can be
simplified dramatically.

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:59.170
So, that's our mission statement,
is really to drive towards

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:03.240
a zero learning curve,
computing experience, whatever that is.

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:07.000
My grandmother,
we bought her an iPad, and,

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:12.320
of course, this was the first time she was
able to adopt computers, or computing.

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:13.821
And what's the killer app for old people?

00:25:13.821 --> 00:25:15.685
&gt;&gt; Facebook.

00:25:15.685 --> 00:25:18.348
&gt;&gt; Solitaire
&gt;&gt; Facebook, correct, close.

00:25:18.348 --> 00:25:20.624
Solitaire was on Windows 95,
but you couldn't get that and

00:25:20.624 --> 00:25:22.307
that's the whole basis for
my speech today.

00:25:22.307 --> 00:25:25.134
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Words With Friends.

00:25:25.134 --> 00:25:25.968
&gt;&gt; Words With Friends,
getting there, warming up.

00:25:25.968 --> 00:25:26.924
&gt;&gt; Scrabble.

00:25:26.924 --> 00:25:28.780
&gt;&gt; Scrabble, even simpler.

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:35.390
Looking at pictures of her grandkids.

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:39.060
So, pinch to zoom, that was
the killer app for my grandma, right?

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:43.810
There's a zero learning curve computer
experience that you just don't have

00:25:43.810 --> 00:25:45.880
in windows 95 before.

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:48.490
So she was able to adopt computing for
the very first time.

00:25:48.490 --> 00:25:53.400
But when we told her grandma, change
the brightness on your iPad, no way.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:58.220
You have to go through a series of
complex menus and buttons and icons,

00:25:58.220 --> 00:26:01.590
abstractions, to get to that point
where you're changing the brightness.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:02.630
She couldn't figure it out.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:06.960
So there's a lot of learning curves still
inside the easiest to use operating system

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.500
in the world, iOS.

00:26:08.500 --> 00:26:13.010
And what we think we can do with AR is
eradicate that learning curve completely

00:26:13.010 --> 00:26:15.500
and make something that
really is as natural and

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:19.120
as simple as that pinch to
zoom across the whole OS.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:21.710
And a set of applications to do that.

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:24.690
The reason I put this girl behind here.

00:26:24.690 --> 00:26:29.340
This four year old came into, this is my
house, and she came into my house and

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:34.620
immediately picked up the headset and the
controllers without talking to anybody.

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.510
She'd never been in it before.

00:26:36.510 --> 00:26:40.130
And with it zero instructions,
she just went to town.

00:26:40.130 --> 00:26:44.940
And I talked to you about this say,
this is your goal to make

00:26:44.940 --> 00:26:49.290
every experience in computing
this easy for people to pick up.

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:52.452
We are going to be talking about this
very application in just a moment,

00:26:52.452 --> 00:26:57.870
tilt brush which I hope you've all
tried because it's freaking awesome.

00:26:57.870 --> 00:27:03.210
And it allows you to draw holographically
in space, in virtual reality.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:08.260
Now this ability of a 2 year old child or
an 80 year old grandfather to just

00:27:08.260 --> 00:27:13.900
pick up the tool and start drawing, start
working is something that is completely

00:27:13.900 --> 00:27:17.780
distinguishable from any
computing paradigm before.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:22.090
&gt;From anything you can do on a Windows or
even an iOS device.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.360
So, we're going to get
to it in just a moment.

00:27:23.360 --> 00:27:30.550
So, that's what we build, builds augmented
reality holographic tools that allow any

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:36.080
thinker or builder or creator to visualize
their imagination and their work in space.

00:27:36.080 --> 00:27:40.700
So, everyone in this room would be
able to use our headset for something.

00:27:40.700 --> 00:27:43.480
And we can get into the specifics for
that in just a moment.

00:27:45.080 --> 00:27:50.990
So Jennifer asked me to focus
on how we create an open culture

00:27:50.990 --> 00:27:56.820
that is empathetic to the market and
to our employees and our partners.

00:27:56.820 --> 00:27:58.840
So I have two examples of this.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:03.650
The first is,
us opening our offices this quarter.

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:08.660
So we are about 150
people now at San Mateo.

00:28:08.660 --> 00:28:11.470
We took over GoPro's old office space.

00:28:11.470 --> 00:28:15.980
And for those of you who might have
seen my TED talk from last year,

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:19.240
we promised a pretty bombastic promise.

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:23.810
And that was that we were going to
by TED of 2017 throw away all of our

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:27.280
external monitors,
essentially throw away our monitors.

00:28:27.280 --> 00:28:31.577
Our beloved multi-screen
station into the garbage and

00:28:31.577 --> 00:28:34.920
replacing it with an AR experience.

00:28:34.920 --> 00:28:37.730
So, why did we do this?

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:43.600
We did this because there's a sensation
in the market that there's,

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.580
that AR and VR, specially AR,

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:50.776
are vapor wear, that it's not real,
that it's far away from being useful.

00:28:50.776 --> 00:28:58.060
And by us embodying and
really trying out these use cases and

00:28:58.060 --> 00:29:02.860
being the first AR enabled office space in
the world, we were hoping to dispel that.

00:29:02.860 --> 00:29:07.020
Now you might have heard of some
woes that a competitor of ours has

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:12.000
gotten into in the last few months,
because of over hyping technology and

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:16.080
not being there when it
comes to the actual product.

00:29:16.080 --> 00:29:22.280
So we're hoping to undo that, and
bring in people into our company.

00:29:22.280 --> 00:29:25.830
So this openness is very literal,
we're inviting anyone,

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:30.710
any academic, any journalist,
anyone to come over to our offices and

00:29:30.710 --> 00:29:34.860
experience what it might look like
to be inside of augmented reality

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:37.910
exclusively without
a traditional computer.

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:40.120
And that should be a really
exciting quarter for

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:43.480
us, as we throw away all of our monitors.

00:29:43.480 --> 00:29:48.323
And that means everyone,
the engineer, my assistant, everyone.

00:29:48.323 --> 00:29:51.039
So that's the first way,
the second way is,

00:29:51.039 --> 00:29:54.400
remember we're focused in
on this operating system.

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:58.880
This holy grail of computing
that will simplify things for

00:29:58.880 --> 00:30:01.510
grandmothers across the land.

00:30:01.510 --> 00:30:06.741
Now we believe the pathway to that
is by understanding how grandma and

00:30:06.741 --> 00:30:09.188
grandpa process information.

00:30:09.188 --> 00:30:11.150
In other words, neuroscience.

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:15.940
So by understanding cognitive neuroscience
is fairly in-depth, and creating

00:30:15.940 --> 00:30:20.898
a set of simple design guidelines,
we believe we can create that experience.

00:30:20.898 --> 00:30:25.880
The Tilt Brush experience and create
an infinite number of applications that

00:30:25.880 --> 00:30:30.050
feel as simple as that
computing experience.

00:30:30.050 --> 00:30:33.860
So I'd like to tell you about
a couple of those principles.

00:30:36.560 --> 00:30:40.720
But before I do, I'll just say that we're
open sourcing our design guidelines for

00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:42.350
designing this OS.

00:30:42.350 --> 00:30:45.280
And that's another way we're trying
to open up to the community and

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.640
you might ask,
why on earth would you do that?

00:30:48.640 --> 00:30:53.700
And, if you're focused in such
a crowded complex ecosystem is on

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:57.100
the operating system, why would you
tell your competitors how to build it.

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:02.660
The answer is, like in virtual reality,
if one person gets a negative

00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:06.390
experience it tends to reflect
on the rest of the industry.

00:31:06.390 --> 00:31:10.230
So my hope is that our competitors will
look into these design guidelines and

00:31:10.230 --> 00:31:10.790
will try and

00:31:10.790 --> 00:31:15.320
make more delightful computing experiences
that will uplift the whole industry.

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:18.880
So that's the design guideline.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:24.638
Let's talk about just two of them and
then we'll wrap up and pass the mic back.

00:31:24.638 --> 00:31:27.843
So Tilt Brush, so so cool, right.

00:31:27.843 --> 00:31:32.560
And our competitor comes from originally,
Oculus had a demo and now Vibe.

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:35.780
&gt;&gt; It's made by Google, yeah.

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:40.020
&gt;&gt; It was made by Skillman and
Hackett that were bought by Google.

00:31:40.020 --> 00:31:46.270
And look at the transmitting
of imagination into creation.

00:31:46.270 --> 00:31:48.380
In a sense,
there's no latency between the two,

00:31:48.380 --> 00:31:52.900
we've increased the I/O between man and
machine with this kind of an application.

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:55.550
And I think that with a set of about

00:31:55.550 --> 00:32:00.370
ten simple easy to understand design
guidelines, anyone can build a Tilt Brush.

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:03.920
And tiltbrush is considered in
many circles the killer app of VR,

00:32:03.920 --> 00:32:07.540
its a driving adoption in many
senses of virtual reality.

00:32:07.540 --> 00:32:12.664
Even more than the games that people
thought would drive adoption.

00:32:12.664 --> 00:32:16.029
So two little stories on
how to build a tiltbrush.

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:21.720
The first is our principle which
is called minimize abstractions.

00:32:21.720 --> 00:32:28.090
So, minimize abstractions has to do with
our philosophy around spatial computing.

00:32:28.090 --> 00:32:32.840
Spatial graphical user interface, we
believe is the next 50 years of computing.

00:32:32.840 --> 00:32:37.840
It's going to engulf every AR and
VR company that is trying to build

00:32:37.840 --> 00:32:41.320
an operating system will adopt
roughly the spatial computing.

00:32:41.320 --> 00:32:43.190
So, let's go ahead and define it.

00:32:43.190 --> 00:32:47.230
Before we do, let's define what it's
changing, what it's disrupting.

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:50.699
This is the Windows
graphical user interface,

00:32:50.699 --> 00:32:55.034
sort of summarized by the acronym WIMP,
Windows, Icons,

00:32:55.034 --> 00:32:59.036
Menus and Pointers,
created in Xerox PARC in '72.

00:32:59.036 --> 00:33:04.135
And then Steve Jobs pulled it
into Apple for the Mac and

00:33:04.135 --> 00:33:10.070
then Gates took it into Windows 95,
iOS took it back.

00:33:10.070 --> 00:33:14.570
Fundamentally all of these operating
systems are about a metaphorical window

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:19.490
being able to close it being able to see
your home screen, iconography, menus.

00:33:19.490 --> 00:33:23.150
That's what the last 50
years of computing is.

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:28.770
That's where your imaginations have been
trapped in the last 50 years of computing.

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:31.410
And what we're hoping is
that the next 50 years,

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:34.260
we'll free them like that
tiltbrush experience.

00:33:34.260 --> 00:33:37.340
And here's a particular
way that we can do this.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:39.760
The first principle we
call minimize obstruction.

00:33:39.760 --> 00:33:43.000
So if in the old world that was
about menus and windows and

00:33:43.000 --> 00:33:46.658
all this complexity, the new world is
about two things and two things alone.

00:33:46.658 --> 00:33:50.300
Tools and content.

00:33:50.300 --> 00:33:54.070
That's it, that's all the animals
that matter in the new world.

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:57.480
So let's imagine Photoshop of the past,
right?

00:33:57.480 --> 00:34:01.603
Photoshop of the past,
it's riddled with all this abstraction,

00:34:01.603 --> 00:34:06.329
different layering systems, side panels,
crowding on top of each other.

00:34:06.329 --> 00:34:09.924
How long does it take me to get to
the damn paint brush and customize it?

00:34:09.924 --> 00:34:14.723
By the time I got there I got burnt out, I
don't have a creation, I can't do that for

00:34:14.723 --> 00:34:16.660
sure as a novice in Photoshop.

00:34:16.660 --> 00:34:19.670
That's the old world, but
what's the new world look like

00:34:19.670 --> 00:34:23.720
when it comes just to tools and
content and AR or VR?

00:34:23.720 --> 00:34:29.130
Well, it's as simple as seeing that
paintbrush, picking it up, and

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:34.330
maybe putting on a different header to it,
dipping it in some paint,

00:34:34.330 --> 00:34:35.950
and starting with the paint.

00:34:35.950 --> 00:34:42.160
And that process is where computing
is going, into tools and content.

00:34:42.160 --> 00:34:43.360
Those are the two buckets.

00:34:43.360 --> 00:34:44.810
So, how do we design these tools?

00:34:44.810 --> 00:34:46.480
We're talking about designing for AR VR.

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:48.639
I'll wrap up in just about a minute.

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:51.270
We design them with affordances.

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:54.790
We take cues from
the industrial design world.

00:34:54.790 --> 00:35:00.470
Affordances are these things
like a handle for a teacup or

00:35:00.470 --> 00:35:07.468
the little nooks on the side of a hammer
where it tells me how to grab it.

00:35:07.468 --> 00:35:13.638
These things essentially tell
the brain how to grab it.

00:35:13.638 --> 00:35:16.300
Now, Professor in 2001,

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:21.150
discovered, and all of our guidelines
are anchored on neuroscience.

00:35:21.150 --> 00:35:28.380
He discovered that there's this area
called R4, sorry, F4, in the motor cortex.

00:35:28.380 --> 00:35:31.260
Which is controlling
the movement of the hand.

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:34.660
That looks at objects,
at the characteristics of objects, and

00:35:34.660 --> 00:35:40.430
reverse engineers those
characteristics to create an automatic

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:44.930
series of motor commands to allow you
to reach out and grab the object.

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:47.340
So the object is telling
you how to use it.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:49.520
That's what we're talking about.

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:53.287
That's how you eradicate
the learning curve from computing.

00:35:53.287 --> 00:35:59.523
So that's one way in which we're going to
create a bunch of tiltbrushes, together.

00:35:59.523 --> 00:36:04.200
The other way is what we called augmented,
not mixed, reality.

00:36:04.200 --> 00:36:07.820
Robert likes this term mixed reality and
I don't, so

00:36:07.820 --> 00:36:10.556
today we'll have a little bit of a debate.

00:36:10.556 --> 00:36:14.880
So Professor asked me

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:19.760
to imagine the future with you guys,
and conclude this talk.

00:36:19.760 --> 00:36:25.400
So I want you to imagine yourself in
about five years waking up in bed.

00:36:25.400 --> 00:36:28.780
And rolling over and reaching for
that familiar thing.

00:36:28.780 --> 00:36:34.580
Hopefully for our shareholders that's not
an iPhone, that's a pair of AR glasses.

00:36:34.580 --> 00:36:35.910
And how do these glasses look?

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:38.930
They look like a strip of
glass that's nearly invisible.

00:36:38.930 --> 00:36:43.637
&gt;From the distance that we're standing from
each other they're even less of a visual

00:36:43.637 --> 00:36:45.237
footprint than my glasses.

00:36:45.237 --> 00:36:50.798
You put the strip of glass on and you can
see photorealistic light field holograms.

00:36:50.798 --> 00:36:55.090
My friends, we're working on these
kinds of glasses, magically, Microsoft,

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:59.350
everyone is, and this hardware I
promise you will soon be commoditized.

00:36:59.350 --> 00:37:02.240
So when yo talk about getting
away from talking about specs and

00:37:02.240 --> 00:37:05.340
obsessing on the pixel size,
that's wise because

00:37:05.340 --> 00:37:08.880
what the important thing is what
you put inside of these glasses.

00:37:08.880 --> 00:37:10.170
Every little nuance and

00:37:10.170 --> 00:37:14.530
difference is going to change the lives
of everyone adopting this afterwards.

00:37:14.530 --> 00:37:20.350
I was just sitting with Scott Forstall
in my office a couple of days ago.

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:23.510
Scott was the designer of iOS.

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:26.730
He essentially is responsible for
a lot of the iPhone.

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:31.620
And he's looking around in SFO at
an army of people just hunching over and

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:40.110
he says it's really upsetting what world
we've brought forward, what we've created.

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:42.065
&gt;&gt; Hey, at least we're not
locked to our desks anymore.

00:37:42.065 --> 00:37:43.930
[LAUGH].

00:37:43.930 --> 00:37:45.880
&gt;&gt; Fair enough.

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:49.830
I told him I think we've turned into
a species of phone monkeys, but

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:51.640
that's just my theory.

00:37:51.640 --> 00:37:54.950
So, I said, yes, we gotta fix this,
and this is how we can fix it.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:56.870
We have a few different ways.

00:37:56.870 --> 00:38:00.090
So you put on the strip of glass,
back into that morning.

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:05.130
And, the first thing you see is
an explosion on the side of your wall,

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:08.430
just like you saw in
this hollow lens thing.

00:38:08.430 --> 00:38:12.320
And a dinosaur comes rushing in and
this dinosaur is photo realistic and that

00:38:12.320 --> 00:38:16.390
hole looks is real as can be and it sure
as hell wakes you up better than a coffee.

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:20.870
And this dinosaur's running through
the room with a notification in its mouth,

00:38:20.870 --> 00:38:22.340
did you forget milk this morning?

00:38:24.050 --> 00:38:25.625
Notification from your wife.

00:38:25.625 --> 00:38:28.670
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Given through the dinosaur.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:30.517
I don't know, I'm just making this UI up.

00:38:30.517 --> 00:38:32.820
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So this is option A.

00:38:32.820 --> 00:38:34.340
It's called mixed reality.

00:38:34.340 --> 00:38:38.690
Mixed reality means to warp a part
of your reality like a wall and

00:38:38.690 --> 00:38:42.140
blast the hole through it and
change it to warp it.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:44.660
Let's imagine a different reality.

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:48.710
You put on your headset, nothing happens
until you want it to, you get up.

00:38:48.710 --> 00:38:54.060
You go to Crystal Lake, you take a little
bit of a jog by the side of the lake and

00:38:54.060 --> 00:38:56.850
on the side you see a flower
that kind of attracts you.

00:38:56.850 --> 00:39:00.790
It's interesting and you touch it,
and right next to it comes a meta user

00:39:00.790 --> 00:39:04.610
interface, a meta UI like metadata,
that's how I'm using the word meta now.

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:06.810
That just tells you,

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.740
it's a Wiki panel that tells you how
much DNA you share with the flower.

00:39:11.980 --> 00:39:16.800
So, it enriches, it deepens
the relationship between you and

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:18.190
the world, okay?

00:39:18.190 --> 00:39:21.070
And then you keep jogging,
keep going on your jogging.

00:39:21.070 --> 00:39:23.050
You see someone at a distance and

00:39:23.050 --> 00:39:28.210
that person is from this class,
from Jennifer's class.

00:39:28.210 --> 00:39:31.550
And you didn't know that because
the jogger's at a distance.

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:34.280
And in turns out that you could
see with a panel right next

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:35.600
to them that they can also see, so

00:39:35.600 --> 00:39:41.080
it's not creepy like Google Glass that
they are also Grateful Dead fans like you.

00:39:42.110 --> 00:39:46.610
So by the time they arrive, by the time
you cross paths, you can reach out,

00:39:46.610 --> 00:39:51.150
shake their hand, become friends on
Facebook perhaps, and continue the run.

00:39:51.150 --> 00:39:55.400
This is another example of a meta
UI that deepens your connection

00:39:55.400 --> 00:39:58.420
With the real world and with each other,
people in the real world.

00:39:58.420 --> 00:40:01.900
So I want to create that world.

00:40:01.900 --> 00:40:06.244
A world were it's not distorted,
it's not manipulated but

00:40:06.244 --> 00:40:11.122
it's rather augmented and
that's why I love this term augmented.

00:40:11.122 --> 00:40:14.150
So that's our last principle
called augmented not mixed.

00:40:14.150 --> 00:40:18.030
There's a lot of neuroscience to suggest
why it's better to not have dinosaurs

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:22.585
breaking through walls, and
that change all of your, what we call,

00:40:22.585 --> 00:40:26.160
Bayesian priors of your brain,
your mental model of your brain.

00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:29.820
All the time can change if
there's no laws of physics.

00:40:29.820 --> 00:40:33.620
And that can create sort
of delusional thinking or

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:35.730
anxiety around what's
going to happen next.

00:40:35.730 --> 00:40:40.060
So there's a lot of neuroscience behind
all these things but I just wanted

00:40:40.060 --> 00:40:44.540
to give you a taste of what we think
about everyday and why is this important?

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:48.743
Well, because the next 50 years of
computing are going to be anchored on

00:40:48.743 --> 00:40:49.593
the next iOS.

00:40:49.593 --> 00:40:53.074
And our kids are going to grow up inside
of these things and our kids kids so

00:40:53.074 --> 00:40:55.493
we want to make sure they
have a comfortable stay.

00:40:55.493 --> 00:40:56.335
Thank you.

00:40:56.335 --> 00:41:02.000
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Awesome, come on up.

00:41:02.000 --> 00:41:03.011
Yeah, that was awesome.

00:41:03.011 --> 00:41:03.863
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:41:05.689 --> 00:41:08.205
&gt;&gt; How do I add more
value to you right now?

00:41:08.205 --> 00:41:15.600
You're getting a taste in this class,
over the next few months, you are going to

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:19.510
probably hear from some of the people that
built some of the stuff I've showed you.

00:41:19.510 --> 00:41:24.400
I just came back from Sundance and
I saw films from as crazy

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:29.034
an experience that started out
with looking at a gay sex act and

00:41:29.034 --> 00:41:34.670
VR to Chris Mill's thing where
you're flying through space with

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:41.320
another person is quite amazing.

00:41:41.320 --> 00:41:47.000
How do you think these
systems will philosophically

00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:52.020
differ from each other because that I
sense that you have a deeper philosophical

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:55.690
difference from some of the other
people who are building these systems.

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:59.340
Because let's to go dystopian,
there's a Japan,

00:41:59.340 --> 00:42:04.690
in fact I should pull up the Japanese
video where he puts ads on every,

00:42:04.690 --> 00:42:11.200
this Japanese filmmaker made a video where
he pretends we're in this new world,

00:42:11.200 --> 00:42:15.470
and he puts ads and other information
on every surface around him.

00:42:15.470 --> 00:42:18.860
And sounds like you don't
like that kind of future.

00:42:18.860 --> 00:42:22.390
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I hope we don't all like
that or we all don't like that future.

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:25.580
But yes, that's the future we're
trying to protect against.

00:42:27.986 --> 00:42:32.690
So there will be philosophical differences
that are inherent in the form factor.

00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:37.950
Virtual reality seeks to pull you into
another world, maybe deepen the connection

00:42:37.950 --> 00:42:42.680
with other people inside of that world,
in this mediation phase.

00:42:42.680 --> 00:42:48.540
But mixed reality seeks to stimulate
you by changing the characteristics

00:42:48.540 --> 00:42:53.680
of the world and augmented is simply a way
to enrich your life with more information

00:42:53.680 --> 00:42:59.510
about the real world, real people in the
real world and objects in the real world.

00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:04.770
I'm excited about that and I think when we
think about the far future it's going to

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.805
go inside of glasses and then who knows,

00:43:06.805 --> 00:43:10.290
brain computer interfaces in the future,
who knows where it's going to go.

00:43:10.290 --> 00:43:14.940
But as we become more intimately connected
with the machine we want to make sure that

00:43:14.940 --> 00:43:16.280
it's a great experience.

00:43:16.280 --> 00:43:20.090
Because it's so
easy to manipulate someones thoughts.

00:43:20.090 --> 00:43:24.828
And we show the neuroscience of how
easy it is to generate delusions in

00:43:24.828 --> 00:43:29.990
a healthy person's mind, that we think
we have a bit of a responsibility.

00:43:29.990 --> 00:43:31.520
&gt;&gt; We should talk about these downsides.

00:43:31.520 --> 00:43:34.100
In my book we have a whole
chapter on possible downsides,

00:43:34.100 --> 00:43:37.250
control of people,
delusions, and other things.

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:38.590
We should talk about that.

00:43:38.590 --> 00:43:40.460
The upside is this is a drug.

00:43:40.460 --> 00:43:43.745
I've been telling my friends
this is LSD for everybody.

00:43:43.745 --> 00:43:46.658
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; because if I want that wall to be

00:43:46.658 --> 00:43:49.653
a multicolored array of ants,
I can make it happen.

00:43:49.653 --> 00:43:53.210
In fact, I'm starting to write a list
of rules of this new mixed reality

00:43:53.210 --> 00:43:53.922
role world and

00:43:53.922 --> 00:43:58.005
the first rule is there's nothing you can
tell me about this world that is false.

00:43:58.005 --> 00:43:59.480
[LAUGH] Visually at least.

00:43:59.480 --> 00:44:00.230
&gt;&gt; Right, right.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:04.230
&gt;&gt; I thought it was going to be no
multicolored hands, the first rule.

00:44:04.230 --> 00:44:07.400
&gt;&gt; No multicolored, no, whatever you want
to put on that wall, you can make it.

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:11.320
&gt;&gt; The key is, Robert, it's fine for
you to pull the multicolored answer but

00:44:11.320 --> 00:44:14.990
you the user have to be in control and
this is the nuance.

00:44:16.030 --> 00:44:19.080
Thus far the developer's the god and

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:24.270
what we're trying to make sure of as the
developer gets a monopoly over your whole

00:44:24.270 --> 00:44:28.600
field of view is that you become
the controller of your own destiny.

00:44:28.600 --> 00:44:32.990
I want to put the ants on,
that's my UI, that's my app.

00:44:32.990 --> 00:44:35.420
But don't have that
automatically be part of the OS.

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:37.190
Big no-no, I think.

00:44:37.190 --> 00:44:38.640
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a slippery slope though.

00:44:38.640 --> 00:44:42.640
I mean, you look at the majority
of the revenue from the major

00:44:42.640 --> 00:44:47.280
tech companies in the valley, now the new
breed, Google, Facebook is advertising.

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:51.386
And so if these are going to be the
companies that are the vanguard of these

00:44:51.386 --> 00:44:54.840
new technologies, whether they be
AR of VR or MR or whatever that is.

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:58.102
How are they not going to
cater towards that?

00:44:58.102 --> 00:45:00.670
It's where 97 plus percent
of their revenue comes from.

00:45:00.670 --> 00:45:04.760
So when they say, it's very reasonable for
me to put a little ad there and

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:08.042
then charge you less for
your head set that's a step down the road.

00:45:08.042 --> 00:45:10.060
And then when they say
it's very reasonable for

00:45:10.060 --> 00:45:12.550
me to have you watch an ad before
you put on your headsets so

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:14.860
it's a little cheaper,
that's another step down the road.

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:19.670
So I think it's going to be a big
challenge because so much of the economy

00:45:19.670 --> 00:45:22.710
of Internet companies, the tech companies,
is built upon advertising.

00:45:22.710 --> 00:45:25.530
And so much of those companies
are the ones who are spending

00:45:25.530 --> 00:45:27.830
hundreds of millions and billions of
dollars on developing these technologies.

00:45:27.830 --> 00:45:29.590
&gt;&gt; That should really alarm us.

00:45:29.590 --> 00:45:32.030
&gt;&gt; It should but it doesn't, right?

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:34.592
We deal with that at Facebook,
we deal with that at Twitter,

00:45:34.592 --> 00:45:37.024
we deal with that in social
media we use every single day.

00:45:37.024 --> 00:45:41.763
&gt;&gt; Here I visited Sephora's R&amp;D lab and
they showed me an augmented makeup

00:45:41.763 --> 00:45:46.656
Where [COUGH] with your iPhone today,
you can aim your iPhone at your face and

00:45:46.656 --> 00:45:51.090
try on different kinds of makeup,
lipsticks and what not.

00:45:51.090 --> 00:45:54.560
And the color is matched
to the physical product.

00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:55.760
So when you got to Sephora and

00:45:55.760 --> 00:46:00.410
actually buy the real makeup it's
matched to what you see on the screen.

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:05.189
And imagine I have these glasses on,
now you could be a KISS character or

00:46:05.189 --> 00:46:07.990
some weird made-up character, right.

00:46:09.410 --> 00:46:11.610
It's going to be crazy in this new world.

00:46:11.610 --> 00:46:15.480
I don't know that your philosophy
is going to completely win.

00:46:15.480 --> 00:46:18.050
because we're going to try a lot of things
and some of them are going to work.

00:46:18.050 --> 00:46:20.009
&gt;&gt; To be honest, it's not a philosophy and

00:46:20.009 --> 00:46:23.188
this is what we're trying to
really document in this guideline.

00:46:23.188 --> 00:46:27.370
We're just trying to find out how
the brain likes to process information.

00:46:27.370 --> 00:46:32.450
And if the brain, and
there's many examples of this, but

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:38.270
if the brain wants to put augmented
costume and makeup on you right now for

00:46:38.270 --> 00:46:42.734
whatever task then that's what
it should be allowed to do.

00:46:42.734 --> 00:46:47.995
The problem is when the brain,
when a developer decides that, and then I,

00:46:47.995 --> 00:46:53.260
a simple user of MR,
will not know if that is real or not real.

00:46:53.260 --> 00:46:55.812
And then it becomes a slippery slope to,

00:46:55.812 --> 00:46:58.899
am I talking to scobo here or
am I talking to an AI?

00:46:58.899 --> 00:47:04.960
Am I talking to some kind of an avatar
that's completely sort of realistic here?

00:47:04.960 --> 00:47:08.840
Should I shake your hand or
will it just go through air?

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:11.750
These kinds of confusions,
they're just simply mental confusions.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:16.200
And we want to make sure that we have
a guideline that allows for that beautiful

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:20.250
crazy future, but makes sure that
we're protected as we go in there.

00:47:20.250 --> 00:47:21.275
That's our goal.

00:47:21.275 --> 00:47:25.530
&gt;&gt; Talked about protection,
I'm sexual abuse victim so

00:47:25.530 --> 00:47:28.190
I care about this topic very deeply.

00:47:28.190 --> 00:47:34.700
When we are in virtual social spaces there
will be bad actors in that social space.

00:47:34.700 --> 00:47:38.055
How do you protect people from that
kind of activity in one of these spaces?

00:47:38.055 --> 00:47:40.395
And both of you, take that on.

00:47:40.395 --> 00:47:43.140
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a huge challenge because

00:47:43.140 --> 00:47:45.930
if the whole point of your product is to
make you feel like you're there with other

00:47:45.930 --> 00:47:48.710
people you have to choose what
people you're there with.

00:47:48.710 --> 00:47:54.150
The early days of virtual reality and
to set context, there's probably

00:47:54.150 --> 00:47:58.822
Somewhere between 1 and 2 million people
a month that use virtual reality headsets,

00:47:58.822 --> 00:48:03.494
in the brackets that matter, not in the
Google Glass, Google Cardboard, excuse me,

00:48:03.494 --> 00:48:04.471
frame of things.

00:48:04.471 --> 00:48:05.914
Those are sort of irrelevant.

00:48:05.914 --> 00:48:08.410
If I use them for
30 seconds that's a long session.

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:12.370
We are talking about Samsung's Gear VR,
and Oculus Rift, and HTC Vive,

00:48:12.370 --> 00:48:13.280
and Google Daydream.

00:48:14.620 --> 00:48:17.250
If the whole point of those products is
to make you feel like you are with other

00:48:17.250 --> 00:48:19.980
people, if you are with
the wrong people that can be

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:21.280
an extremely negative experience.

00:48:21.280 --> 00:48:23.520
You do have this concept
of personal space.

00:48:23.520 --> 00:48:26.940
And you do have this concept where people
have hands and when those hands come

00:48:26.940 --> 00:48:29.740
towards you, you feel like someone's
hand is coming towards you.

00:48:29.740 --> 00:48:33.060
You have the same visceral emotional
reaction like this, which is a good one.

00:48:33.060 --> 00:48:35.661
This is a good one.

00:48:35.661 --> 00:48:38.520
But if it's a stranger,
that's not necessarily a good feeling.

00:48:38.520 --> 00:48:43.135
And so where we are is in this challenging
place where we, I can just say for

00:48:43.135 --> 00:48:47.168
us as a company, is the social
bureaucratic people use a lot is.

00:48:47.168 --> 00:48:50.341
Is that we're trying to learn what
makes a good user interaction

00:48:50.341 --> 00:48:54.470
in a world where most users don't actually
know anybody else that has a VR headset.

00:48:54.470 --> 00:48:57.400
I mean the probability that
your friend has a VR headset so

00:48:57.400 --> 00:49:01.650
you can hang out in our product or any
other one is diminishing small right now.

00:49:01.650 --> 00:49:04.520
So by definition you have groups of
people who don't know each other and

00:49:04.520 --> 00:49:06.190
so you have this possibility for
interaction.

00:49:06.190 --> 00:49:08.230
Now what happens is again,

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:11.180
we try to, you're mirroring sort
of what happens in real life.

00:49:11.180 --> 00:49:14.785
But the problem is in real life you got
a police force, and you got somebody's

00:49:14.785 --> 00:49:17.744
cousin is going to punch you in
the nose if you do something wrong.

00:49:17.744 --> 00:49:19.220
They don't necessarily have that in VR.

00:49:19.220 --> 00:49:21.560
So you don't have this same
social rules that you have there.

00:49:21.560 --> 00:49:25.330
So you need to design them in about
how you bring people together and

00:49:25.330 --> 00:49:27.350
what tools you allow people to say,
okay yeah you,

00:49:27.350 --> 00:49:29.870
you're out of here, and
just get rid of them instantly.

00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:31.420
It's a challenging and
interesting problem, and

00:49:31.420 --> 00:49:35.710
it's not unlike the problems that
Twitter has been totally unable to

00:49:35.710 --> 00:49:39.545
address on their platform but
to a different

00:49:39.545 --> 00:49:42.615
level because of the psychological
experience of that connection.

00:49:42.615 --> 00:49:45.915
&gt;&gt; Is it true in your system,
it might be your system or somebody elses.

00:49:45.915 --> 00:49:49.650
But if I try to touch you inappropriately,
my hand turns around toward me?

00:49:49.650 --> 00:49:51.345
&gt;&gt; It just disappears the way we do it,
yeah.

00:49:51.345 --> 00:49:55.165
So disappears for you, it disappears for
everyone else so no one can see it.

00:49:55.165 --> 00:49:58.675
Some systems have designed to where,
I try to move my hand toward you and

00:49:58.675 --> 00:49:59.980
you don't see it.

00:49:59.980 --> 00:50:02.890
But everybody else in the room sees
me harassing you in some way which

00:50:02.890 --> 00:50:04.070
doesn't solve the problem we think.

00:50:04.070 --> 00:50:08.852
So the harder way to solve that was to
make sure it disappears for everybody.

00:50:08.852 --> 00:50:10.726
It sounds like a simple design thing,
right, but

00:50:10.726 --> 00:50:13.872
it's actually a very challenging
design and technical problem.

00:50:13.872 --> 00:50:19.840
&gt;&gt; Designing this world of etiquette
in virtual reality is super,

00:50:19.840 --> 00:50:20.730
super interesting.

00:50:22.250 --> 00:50:26.694
In augmented reality,
there's a similar question because,

00:50:26.694 --> 00:50:32.100
assume for a moment the term augmented
reality covers a lot of things,

00:50:32.100 --> 00:50:33.960
including even Google Glass.

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:37.760
When we had Glass,
people were looking at each other and

00:50:37.760 --> 00:50:40.830
they weren't really sure what
was happening on the other side.

00:50:40.830 --> 00:50:45.990
There were these asymmetrical UIs that I
believe was the downfall of the product.

00:50:45.990 --> 00:50:49.150
More than the look,
which was geeky, that's fine.

00:50:49.150 --> 00:50:51.910
The form factor is less of a concern for
me.

00:50:51.910 --> 00:50:54.190
It's the fact that I just don't know
what you're looking at right now.

00:50:54.190 --> 00:50:57.490
I don't know if you're recording it but
me, right now, by design,

00:50:57.490 --> 00:50:59.260
they actually designed it that way.

00:50:59.260 --> 00:51:02.860
So, one of our principles,
and the brain has so

00:51:02.860 --> 00:51:06.570
much processing around attentional cues.

00:51:06.570 --> 00:51:10.561
Like, if you're looking at me right now, I
have this area in my medial temporal lobe

00:51:10.561 --> 00:51:13.218
called a fusiform gyrus which
is analyzing the whites-

00:51:13.218 --> 00:51:14.970
&gt;&gt; Wait, slow down what did you just say?

00:51:14.970 --> 00:51:19.960
&gt;&gt; I have this area that is analyzing your
facial features to know if you're paying

00:51:19.960 --> 00:51:21.750
attention to me.

00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:26.410
To know if you're aroused by
whatever I'm saying right now, etc.

00:51:26.410 --> 00:51:31.330
And when you use your hands there's
this freaky cool thing which is that

00:51:31.330 --> 00:51:35.361
the same area in your brain,
if you were to move your hands like this,

00:51:35.361 --> 00:51:39.300
are now firing by you looking
at me moving my hands.

00:51:39.300 --> 00:51:40.210
So we're matched.

00:51:40.210 --> 00:51:44.560
There's this social
underpinning of empathy.

00:51:44.560 --> 00:51:47.065
So our principle bottom line for

00:51:47.065 --> 00:51:51.390
etiquette inside of AR is that everything
has to be viewed by everyone else.

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:54.560
It's public by default,
this is one of our principles.

00:51:54.560 --> 00:51:57.735
So, let's warm up,
I'm trying to spice it up guys.

00:51:57.735 --> 00:51:59.235
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Come on work with me here.

00:51:59.235 --> 00:52:00.500
&gt;&gt; Because if it's
&gt;&gt; That sounds crazy.

00:52:00.500 --> 00:52:01.490
I want privacy.

00:52:01.490 --> 00:52:03.360
All right, let's talk about it.

00:52:03.360 --> 00:52:06.910
&gt;&gt; If it is public and I believe that by
the end of the year, we're going to see

00:52:06.910 --> 00:52:11.800
several companies bring out a new
kind of 3D map based on Slam, right?

00:52:12.850 --> 00:52:16.730
Where the glasses are going to see this
world and are going to remember it.

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:19.570
Right, if I use my HoloLens,
I can put a shark here.

00:52:19.570 --> 00:52:21.410
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And if I come back a year from now,

00:52:21.410 --> 00:52:22.950
that shark will be right where I left it.

00:52:22.950 --> 00:52:23.890
&gt;&gt; Great.

00:52:23.890 --> 00:52:26.646
&gt;&gt; And if I could publicly share
that shark with the class,

00:52:26.646 --> 00:52:29.123
the whole class would see the shark here,
right?

00:52:29.123 --> 00:52:35.970
&gt;&gt; Uh-huh, and that's exactly what we
actually we promote, in this new world.

00:52:35.970 --> 00:52:38.510
We want the world to
feel like an architecture

00:52:38.510 --> 00:52:39.640
office where you come to visit.

00:52:39.640 --> 00:52:41.290
You see all these beautiful creations.

00:52:41.290 --> 00:52:43.120
You can share them with one another.

00:52:43.120 --> 00:52:47.550
And if you want to chuck one,
you just pick it up and throw it away and

00:52:47.550 --> 00:52:48.860
it's gone.

00:52:48.860 --> 00:52:52.594
I think if you imagine yourself here
in a couple of years in this classroom,

00:52:52.594 --> 00:52:54.207
all of us wearing our headsets.

00:52:54.207 --> 00:52:57.007
Just the beautiful colors and

00:52:57.007 --> 00:53:02.910
mise-en-scène around us is
such a cool visual to have.

00:53:02.910 --> 00:53:05.130
And for some of that to be private or

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:09.240
hidden from one another just because it's
my creation and not yours, it deviates so

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:12.290
much from our mental model of the real
world that it's not worth it.

00:53:12.290 --> 00:53:15.480
&gt;&gt; But in my glasses I'm watching stuff
that I might not want you to see.

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:17.234
&gt;&gt; So
do what you're doing in the real world.

00:53:17.234 --> 00:53:18.695
Go to the other room and do it there.

00:53:18.695 --> 00:53:21.160
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's my point though.

00:53:21.160 --> 00:53:23.840
It's the nuance,
it's that etiquette prevails.

00:53:23.840 --> 00:53:28.590
The social norms that we're talking
about that sort of get in to VR and

00:53:28.590 --> 00:53:33.830
control the way we design our UIs will be
the same, I think, for augmented reality.

00:53:33.830 --> 00:53:38.790
But I don't know if you guys have seen
the Blackmere episode with John Ham where

00:53:38.790 --> 00:53:43.990
there's an offender and
one person looking at that offender only

00:53:43.990 --> 00:53:48.910
sees their silhouette and the offender can
never talk, or never communicate back.

00:53:48.910 --> 00:53:50.795
It's called diminished reality.

00:53:50.795 --> 00:53:52.820
This is the opposite of all
that we're talking about.

00:53:54.310 --> 00:53:59.420
And that's the world that, I think, a few
of the competitors are steering towards.

00:53:59.420 --> 00:54:01.930
This is my private thing, I'm going to
create a silhouette around it but

00:54:01.930 --> 00:54:03.260
here's the problem with that.

00:54:03.260 --> 00:54:05.120
When I'm talking to a silhouette,

00:54:05.120 --> 00:54:09.770
when I'm interacting with a silhouette
that you can't see, that confuses you.

00:54:09.770 --> 00:54:13.280
And just creates a lot anxiety and
Google Glass feeling, I think,

00:54:13.280 --> 00:54:15.090
in the room and we want to avoid that.

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:19.206
So, that's one of our little thingys.

00:54:19.206 --> 00:54:22.213
&gt;&gt; At Coachella, big music festival,

00:54:22.213 --> 00:54:26.580
Doppler Labs sold me
augmented hearing headphones.

00:54:26.580 --> 00:54:31.070
And these headphones block the analog
audio from getting to your ear.

00:54:31.070 --> 00:54:33.139
They have microphones on them.

00:54:33.139 --> 00:54:34.830
They have processing,
and they have speaker.

00:54:34.830 --> 00:54:38.510
In fact I have,
I can't show you what I have in my pocket.

00:54:38.510 --> 00:54:41.530
But I have some headphones that
have six microphones on them.

00:54:41.530 --> 00:54:44.750
And they're using artificial
intelligence to find my voice and

00:54:44.750 --> 00:54:46.670
make it better, all sorts of fun stuff.

00:54:48.160 --> 00:54:50.756
These Doppler labs,
they make the audio better.

00:54:50.756 --> 00:54:54.544
They're doing to audio
what you're doing to-

00:54:54.544 --> 00:54:55.085
&gt;&gt; To the world.

00:54:55.085 --> 00:54:57.450
&gt;&gt; To visuals right?

00:54:57.450 --> 00:55:00.081
And when you put them in,
keep in mind you're at Coachella and

00:55:00.081 --> 00:55:02.459
you're in front of $2 million
worth of audio gear, so

00:55:02.459 --> 00:55:05.873
you're having about as good an audio
experience as you're ever going to have.

00:55:05.873 --> 00:55:08.279
Right, cus it's pretty bad ass.

00:55:08.279 --> 00:55:10.805
But you put these stupid
$200 headphones in and

00:55:10.805 --> 00:55:13.575
all of a sudden that sounds
better because it's augmented.

00:55:13.575 --> 00:55:16.934
You can turn down the volume,
you can turn up the bass,

00:55:16.934 --> 00:55:19.310
you can do all sorts of weird effects.

00:55:19.310 --> 00:55:23.720
The noise cancelling on these new
products is unbelievable, right?

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:28.035
On and on, but what's weird is you
take them out and it's sounds better.

00:55:28.035 --> 00:55:29.790
&gt;&gt; You take them out and
the real world sounds better.

00:55:29.790 --> 00:55:31.483
&gt;&gt; The real world sounds better.

00:55:31.483 --> 00:55:33.423
And you put them back in it sounds better.

00:55:33.423 --> 00:55:35.368
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How can it sound better both ways?

00:55:35.368 --> 00:55:36.932
&gt;&gt; It depends what you took at Coachella.

00:55:36.932 --> 00:55:42.589
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Burn, but I was sober with these.

00:55:42.589 --> 00:55:44.728
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He's sober, he's sober.

00:55:44.728 --> 00:55:49.680
&gt;&gt; I don't drink anymore, and I was
not using other things at that point.

00:55:49.680 --> 00:55:54.280
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; And there's a principle here.

00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:59.520
That our human mind likes analog and
likes augmented.

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:00.590
It likes both.

00:56:01.970 --> 00:56:07.842
And Neil Young's audio engineer took
me into his audio lab over here,

00:56:07.842 --> 00:56:12.717
and he played Harvest Moon off
of the two-inch analog tape

00:56:12.717 --> 00:56:16.650
in the studio where it was recorded.

00:56:16.650 --> 00:56:22.177
And then he had a series of devices where
he could show me the digital music,

00:56:22.177 --> 00:56:28.784
all the way down to 44.1 kilohertz sampled
music which is what's on a CD or MP3 file.

00:56:28.784 --> 00:56:33.370
You could really tell the difference
between that and analog.

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:37.100
Anybody who has a vinyl record
collection knows that analog

00:56:37.100 --> 00:56:40.890
sounds better when you have
everything set up perfectly.

00:56:40.890 --> 00:56:45.920
But I don't listen to analog
because it's not convenient, right?

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:50.620
My Spotify on my phone has thousands
of songs on my phone, right?

00:56:50.620 --> 00:56:51.950
&gt;&gt; Here's the thing about convenience.

00:56:51.950 --> 00:56:55.350
When it gets too convenient,
I stop listening to music.

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:58.060
It's like I turn into
a phone monkey again and

00:56:58.060 --> 00:57:03.030
I'm like I just lose track of all of
the plethora colossal CD collection.

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:04.670
And then I just want to go home and
listen.

00:57:04.670 --> 00:57:09.900
I literally just bought Harvest from Neil
Young, not Harvest Moon, in record form.

00:57:09.900 --> 00:57:13.470
And I bought a record player last week and
I feel totally hipster for

00:57:13.470 --> 00:57:18.310
doing this, and odd actually,
because it's so much less convenient.

00:57:18.310 --> 00:57:21.050
But then I find myself sitting there,
isolated in the room, and

00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:25.260
just listening to it and hearing every
little nuance off of the record.

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:26.760
So yes, we love our analog and

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:30.650
I'm the CEO of an AR company so
it's a crazy world that we're going into.

00:57:30.650 --> 00:57:31.630
&gt;&gt; Where I'm going is,

00:57:31.630 --> 00:57:34.850
I believe we're going to take
the glasses off a lot to check in.

00:57:34.850 --> 00:57:37.720
First of all, if you're a supermodel
sitting in front of me right now,

00:57:37.720 --> 00:57:41.010
I'm going to check to make sure,
wait a second, [LAUGH] right?

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:44.190
&gt;&gt; Because you're going to be able to
portray yourself however you want.

00:57:44.190 --> 00:57:47.960
I mean,
we see already augmented reality makeup.

00:57:47.960 --> 00:57:48.929
That ain't going to stop, right?

00:57:48.929 --> 00:57:50.840
&gt;&gt; Nope.
&gt;&gt; If I have a slam based

00:57:50.840 --> 00:57:52.402
mattress-
&gt;&gt; It will make augmented,

00:57:52.402 --> 00:57:53.005
the term augmented literally.

00:57:53.005 --> 00:57:54.306
&gt;&gt; I could get rid of you, right?

00:57:54.306 --> 00:57:56.994
I could erase you from my visual world,
right?

00:57:56.994 --> 00:57:59.810
I could put SpongeBob in it, right?

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:01.418
If I wanted to, you could be SpongeBob.

00:58:01.418 --> 00:58:07.120
So I'm constantly going to be checking
to make sure that the world is accurate,

00:58:07.120 --> 00:58:11.370
right, and
I'm still going to enjoy that analog way.

00:58:11.370 --> 00:58:17.190
We still are a long way from
duplicating the nature's beauty, right?

00:58:17.190 --> 00:58:20.740
You go to Yosemite, you might want
to see that augmented at some

00:58:20.740 --> 00:58:25.690
places to know the names of the mountains
and know the names of the flowers and

00:58:25.690 --> 00:58:27.270
all the stuff you were talking about.

00:58:27.270 --> 00:58:29.500
But we're still going to want to
take the glasses off and

00:58:29.500 --> 00:58:33.480
enjoy the analog wave-
&gt;&gt; I guess I believe in a symbiotic

00:58:33.480 --> 00:58:35.590
future where you don't have
to take off the glasses, but

00:58:35.590 --> 00:58:37.710
they're not trying to fool you.

00:58:37.710 --> 00:58:42.030
And that's the nuance,
that's all that I talk about.

00:58:42.030 --> 00:58:44.565
If you design your UI in
a way that's natural and

00:58:44.565 --> 00:58:48.310
that cares about the beauty of the world,
then you can win.

00:58:48.310 --> 00:58:49.960
&gt;&gt; Let's go deep.

00:58:49.960 --> 00:58:53.420
I have an app on my-
&gt;&gt; Do we still have time?

00:58:53.420 --> 00:58:55.169
I got a two-minute signal across there.

00:58:55.169 --> 00:58:55.669
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Questions?

00:58:55.669 --> 00:58:57.360
&gt;&gt; What?

00:58:57.360 --> 00:59:01.454
&gt;&gt; Okay, sure.

00:59:01.454 --> 00:59:02.372
&gt;&gt; It gets a little messy.

00:59:02.372 --> 00:59:03.138
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:59:03.138 --> 00:59:04.505
I have an app on my HoloLens

00:59:04.505 --> 00:59:05.562
called Fragments,

00:59:05.562 --> 00:59:07.863
where a murder happens right in the room.
So

00:59:07.863 --> 00:59:09.376
it maps out the room.
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:59:09.376 --> 00:59:11.500
&gt;&gt; It finds every surface in the room and

00:59:11.500 --> 00:59:12.780
puts a murder there.

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:17.410
And at one place, there's these artificial
rats that are running around the floor,

00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:20.740
and because it knows the floor's surface,
it runs around your feet.

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:22.824
It knows where your feet are,

00:59:22.824 --> 00:59:25.890
because the feet are different
than the floor surface, right?

00:59:25.890 --> 00:59:29.443
And it's freaky, but it's quite enjoyable
to have that happen to you, right?

00:59:29.443 --> 00:59:30.343
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:59:30.343 --> 00:59:34.877
&gt;&gt; So I'm not sure that humans
are going to resist this stuff the way you

00:59:34.877 --> 00:59:38.220
hope-
&gt;&gt; I don't hope that they will.

00:59:39.900 --> 00:59:44.160
I just hope that the designers of
the operating system of the future

00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:47.700
won't make a rat or
colorful ant operating system.

00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:51.300
But rather that will be left for the,
remember I said there were two animals in

00:59:51.300 --> 00:59:55.280
the future, two UI components that
survive into the next generation?

00:59:55.280 --> 00:59:58.980
Tools and content,
they become the content of the future.

00:59:58.980 --> 01:00:04.420
Build your app, your entertainment-focused
app, around this crime scene and

01:00:04.420 --> 01:00:09.475
let the democratic forces of the web
dictate how many downloads you have and

01:00:09.475 --> 01:00:13.120
how successful you are and
how much VC money you get.

01:00:13.120 --> 01:00:18.600
And let a lot of people enjoy that
experience by their own decision making.

01:00:18.600 --> 01:00:20.259
And that's important.

01:00:20.259 --> 01:00:21.879
&gt;&gt; And you'll get the last minute.

01:00:21.879 --> 01:00:26.708
&gt;&gt; When I'm in AltspaceVR or some of the
other social VR spaces, there's going to

01:00:26.708 --> 01:00:31.612
be infinite rooms or infinite places to
go and visit, whether it's a field and

01:00:31.612 --> 01:00:36.439
there's a rave in the field, or a room
like this where I'm taking a class, or

01:00:36.439 --> 01:00:39.739
an office where I have
multiple monitors around me.

01:00:40.760 --> 01:00:45.629
&gt;&gt; How do I find those,
the coolest place in the future?

01:00:45.629 --> 01:00:50.354
Because I find I struggle
to know where I should go.

01:00:50.354 --> 01:00:51.342
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:00:51.342 --> 01:00:55.630
&gt;&gt; because it's not like this where people
said, you gotta come into a room and

01:00:55.630 --> 01:00:59.930
they direct me and we have lots of
structures to get people into place.

01:00:59.930 --> 01:01:02.450
It's really hard in some
of these social views.

01:01:02.450 --> 01:01:07.127
&gt;&gt; It's a fascinating challenge, because I
think most people's preconception of what

01:01:07.127 --> 01:01:10.148
virtual reality will be is
a contiguous place, right?

01:01:10.148 --> 01:01:15.316
They read all these Snow Crash and
Ready Player One, and these kind of bibles

01:01:15.316 --> 01:01:20.513
of virtual reality universes and
they talk about planets and places you go.

01:01:20.513 --> 01:01:25.037
We just think that's sort of
nonsense because the idea of this

01:01:25.037 --> 01:01:28.170
continuity of a universe is impractical.

01:01:28.170 --> 01:01:30.940
If I want to go to another place in VR,
I should just go there.

01:01:30.940 --> 01:01:34.242
I don't have to pay somebody to walk
into a virtual telephone booth or

01:01:34.242 --> 01:01:36.140
whatever the hell they
do in Ready Player One.

01:01:37.290 --> 01:01:39.814
So we see it more as a model of,
it's the web,

01:01:39.814 --> 01:01:43.213
where the places you go in
Altspace are literally websites.

01:01:43.213 --> 01:01:46.083
You go to a URL and it downloads
all the stuff you need, and so

01:01:46.083 --> 01:01:48.909
the discovery models will be
more like you have on the web.

01:01:48.909 --> 01:01:52.020
You'll find it on social, you'll find
it on search, you'll find it that way.

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:57.933
So we see embracing how the web developed
in allowing our tools to help people

01:01:57.933 --> 01:02:02.612
create these interesting places rather
than sort of enforcing that it must

01:02:02.612 --> 01:02:04.407
somehow map to a real world.

01:02:04.407 --> 01:02:06.600
&gt;&gt; Real fast, what's one or

01:02:06.600 --> 01:02:12.300
two things that students should try
on a Vive or an Oculus or Gear VR?

01:02:12.300 --> 01:02:15.045
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I mean, I think-
&gt;&gt; Other than your own.

01:02:15.045 --> 01:02:16.342
&gt;&gt; Other than our own.

01:02:16.342 --> 01:02:20.507
I think Google Earth is so cool in VR.

01:02:20.507 --> 01:02:23.473
It's on the Vibe, and
you can sort of find where you grew up and

01:02:23.473 --> 01:02:24.856
walk around your hometown.

01:02:24.856 --> 01:02:28.393
And it's just a really,
really great thing for sense of scale.

01:02:28.393 --> 01:02:31.633
My favorite thing honestly
is sort of casual gaming,

01:02:31.633 --> 01:02:34.519
which is totally anathema
to where VR started.

01:02:34.519 --> 01:02:35.600
&gt;&gt; Superhot?

01:02:35.600 --> 01:02:39.550
&gt;&gt; I haven't played Superhot yet,
but I love Fantastic Contraption,

01:02:39.550 --> 01:02:41.060
which is just as cool.

01:02:41.060 --> 01:02:43.739
If I was a kid and I had this I would
just go crazy, I'd be in there all day.

01:02:43.739 --> 01:02:45.457
But there's that concept that VR is for

01:02:45.457 --> 01:02:48.230
hardcore gamers because
that's where it came out of.

01:02:48.230 --> 01:02:50.598
I just think that's not where it's
going to be where it is at all.

01:02:50.598 --> 01:02:52.840
&gt;&gt; All the kids under 13
will have job simulators.

01:02:52.840 --> 01:02:54.133
&gt;&gt; Job simulators, so cool.

01:02:54.133 --> 01:02:56.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's all stuff that, it's not,

01:02:56.430 --> 01:02:59.345
I don't think there's been
a really successful, maybe one or

01:02:59.345 --> 01:03:02.070
two really successful
more hardcore AAA games.

01:03:02.070 --> 01:03:05.900
All the stuff that's really
compelling is just stuff that's

01:03:05.900 --> 01:03:08.790
the VR equivalent of what
an iOS game would be.

01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:11.966
It's just fun, addictive,
lighthearted, whimsical.

01:03:11.966 --> 01:03:14.179
There's some great stuff out there.

01:03:14.179 --> 01:03:15.639
&gt;&gt; Same question for you?

01:03:15.639 --> 01:03:20.762
&gt;&gt; Tilt Brush, anatomy viewers,
I'm such a medical sciences geek that just

01:03:20.762 --> 01:03:26.210
seeing them photorealistically in front
of me, being able to explode a body and

01:03:26.210 --> 01:03:31.032
look really deep into, it is just always
going to make me feel like a kid.

01:03:31.032 --> 01:03:32.955
It's awesome.

01:03:32.955 --> 01:03:38.345
History, microbiology,
freaking looking up at the stars.

01:03:38.345 --> 01:03:40.715
&gt;&gt; When you first put me
into augmented reality,

01:03:40.715 --> 01:03:45.745
it made me cry because I realized how
much my life was about to change.

01:03:46.835 --> 01:03:51.605
Why can I remember that feeling and
the imagery that you showed me?

01:03:51.605 --> 01:03:55.751
&gt;&gt; It's so interesting that a lot
of people tell us I will never,

01:03:55.751 --> 01:03:59.359
ever forget that first 3D
hologram that I looked at.

01:03:59.359 --> 01:04:02.650
The brain remembers novelty.

01:04:02.650 --> 01:04:03.884
It's a novelty machine.

01:04:03.884 --> 01:04:09.180
It's never seen a photorealistic
object that has no matter,

01:04:09.180 --> 01:04:15.810
that has no atoms, only photons,
and that is just jarring.

01:04:15.810 --> 01:04:17.730
And so you'll always remember that.

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:21.630
And now it's up to the developers to
think about how they could use that fact.

01:04:21.630 --> 01:04:26.500
What is the first image you want to
show a billion people in AR or

01:04:26.500 --> 01:04:28.710
in VR, because that will never be erased?

01:04:28.710 --> 01:04:30.116
&gt;&gt; Pokemon.
&gt;&gt; Pokemon.

01:04:30.116 --> 01:04:32.220
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's one way to do it.

01:04:32.220 --> 01:04:35.255
&gt;&gt; I think that's demo number
one on the new iPhone, come on.

01:04:35.255 --> 01:04:36.024
Pokemon Hunter.

01:04:36.024 --> 01:04:39.848
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Man, yeah.

01:04:39.848 --> 01:04:41.466
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

01:04:41.466 --> 01:04:42.055
&gt;&gt; Pokemon, thank you.

01:04:42.055 --> 01:04:43.743
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

01:04:43.743 --> 01:04:44.958
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

01:04:44.958 --> 01:04:46.510
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

01:04:46.510 --> 01:04:52.030
[MUSIC]

